A||||9||KevinR||Electical problem||||9||
Z||000000||KevinR||05-22-2001||10:47 AM||||I have a 86' se 2.8 and the door chime continues to chime with the doors shut and the car running. also the battery drains within 8-14 hrs. everything is off. I have looked for obvious wire damage but could not find any. Has anyone had this problem before? I would really appreciate any input,thanks.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||11:29 AM||||There are two door chimes : twice a second means your ebrake is on, or the car thinks its on - should be a brake light on the dash lit up too.<P>  about once a second means you left your lights on with the engine off - which doesnt make sense if it happens with the engine running - most likely the ebrake cable switch is stuck on.<P>  the battery drains in 10 hours? a fully charged car battery is about 100 AmpHrs - so you have something drawing about 10 amps! thats alot - like two headlights! or the cigarette lighter, rear window defoger? the heater blower maybe?<P>  or its possible your battery is on its last legs, then it could be something smaller, like the trunk lite staying on.<P>  the way to fix the battery problem is to beg or borrow an amp meter that can handle 30 amps or more, pull the lead off the battery and put the amp meter in the circuit, then start pulling fuzes on at a time (and put them back) to see which one makes the meter drop to near zero. <P>  find the fuze and you will know which circuit has something that is stuck on.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||11:31 AM||||another possibility is that your battery cant hold a charge anymore and is going dead all by itself. If you dont see any current flowing when you check it with an ammeter, then your battery is past its useful life.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||05-22-2001||12:22 PM||||for the chimmer, check in Ogre's Cave (link off the main page from here) easiest solution is just to pull it. For the battery drain, how old is it? I left my trunk light on once over night (probrably 8 hours) and it was enough to kill my batter, but that battery was about 5 years old.||||reg||1
Z||000004||KevinR||05-22-2001||01:06 PM||||The brake light is on so that is most likely the reason for the fast chime but the battery is only 6 months old. When I check the circuit do I pull the positive cable off the battery and put the ammeter between the battery and cable?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||03:57 PM||||yes - measuring amps - current - is like measuring the water flowing through a pipe - you have to break the connection (take the red wire off the battery) and make the current flow from the battery, through the meter, then through the red wire to the car where it will return to the black lead on the battery.<P> if you put an ammeter across the two leads of the battery it will tell you how many amps your battery can produce when shorted out - about 400 - it will blow out the meter (or at least blow the fuse inside the meter). Dont do that!<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||04:01 PM||||If the brake light is on and chiming pull up slightly on the ebrake handle. Is it loose or is there resistance.<P>  Its possible the brakes were set when the car was parked and now the cables are stuck rusted in the on position. If the handle is loose when you pull up on it, push the button and pull it up till it meets resistance and see if the ebrake will release.<P>  You may have been driving around with your ebrake stuck on.<P>  if the handle meets resistance right close to the floor, and the car has been sitting for a while, dont pull up on it, in case the cables are rusted up, until you have the time to grease the cables and mess with them if they stick. But if the ebrake is really off, and the light says its on, then you need to fix the ebrake light switch in the handle assembly.||||reg||1
Z||000007||KevinR||05-23-2001||01:53 PM||||The handle is loose when I pull up on it. I think I will have to fix the switch. Thank you.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||02:08 PM||||if the handle is loose, if it feels like you are only lifting the weight of the handle when you pull up on it then your parking brake is ON!<P> pull it up till you cant anymore, then push the button on the end of the handle - that should release the parking brake. When you push the button you should feel the cables pulling the handle back down.<P>but if the cables are rusted up - they wont let go and the handle will be loose still when you let it back down.<P>when the parking brake is OFF it takes a good amount of force to lift the handle, 8 to 10 lbs or more at least.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||02:10 PM||||this is something that is unique to a fiero - since the parking brake handle is by the door, when you pull it up to set the ebrake, the brake rachets on, and then the handle goes back down loose so its not in your way when you get in and out of the car.<P>   so unlike other cars, when the handle is down the brake could be on or off.||||reg||1
